## 4. Rollback plan

The mutating surface is two writes (one new file, one status field), both additive, neither
in the TCB. Rollback is therefore bounded and cannot leave partial state.

| # | Trigger | Rollback action | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|
| **R1** | WU-1 hash ≠ `e7aad7d3…b169f` | **Halt before any write.** Nothing to undo. Re-derive the body from the court artifact by id; do not proceed with an unpinned file. | INTEGRATOR |
| **R2** | WU-2 finds the target already present | **Never clobber.** Halt. Compare hashes: identical ⇒ WU-2 is already satisfied, skip to WU-3; different ⇒ a competing body exists and the court must adjudicate which is DESIGN-frozen. Do not merge, do not pick. | INTEGRATOR → court |
| **R3** | Any WU-3 command exits non-zero | `rm reviews/spikes/sakg-s1-extraction-probe.md`. Do **not** `rmdir reviews/spikes` — it pre-existed and holds `DEBT-0002-spawnability-spike.md` (V6). The tree is then byte-for-byte pre-promotion. If WU-4 already ran, revert the `S-PROBE` status field to its prior value; nothing else in the backlog was touched. | INTEGRATOR |
| **R4** | Founder returns REVERT at `G-S1-GO` | **Not a file rollback.** The probe is the evidence of the falsification test and is retained; `S-PROBE` stays verified because its accept surface is the artifact's existence and shape, not its verdict. REVERT fires the SAEOS-1 §7 falsifier against the *specification*. Deleting the probe here would destroy the record of the test that fired. | founder / court |
| **R5** | Promoted file differs from the frozen body in any byte (e.g. an edited verdict or a re-worded finding) | Delete the promoted copy and halt. Do not patch it forward. An edited body voids the `design_coherent` gate; re-entry is via **DESIGN**, not via a fix-up at INTEGRATE. | INTEGRATOR → DESIGN |

**Diagnostic branch (before invoking R3).** WU-3 failures are expected to be tooling, not
content, because commands 2–4 already pass against these exact bytes (**V4**). If command 2,
3 or 4 fails while `grep -c '^verdict: '` (or the equivalent) on the same file succeeds, the
fault is a `rg` ignore/filtering artifact, not a defect: re-run with `rg -uu` to confirm, and
report the tooling condition — the content is intact and **no rollback is warranted**.
Invoke R3 only once the file itself is shown to be wrong.

**Blast containment.** `reviews/` is outside Trust Zone 0; `constitution/` is untouched, so
no human ratifier, no Adversary review and no amendment path is engaged
(`constitution/README.md`). Rollback is a single `rm` plus, at most, one reverted status
field, and the source of truth (the court artifact) is immutable and independently
retrievable throughout — so no rollback can lose the deliverable.

